`putPage()` uses `page.source_id || 'default'`, which will silently treat an empty-string source id as the default source. That can reintroduce the “writes land under default” class of bugs if an upstream caller accidentally passes `''` (or any other falsy value) instead of a real source id. Prefer nullish-coalescing (`??`) and/or validate that `source_id` is a non-empty string so invalid source IDs fail fast rather than falling back to `default`.
```suggestion
    const sourceId = page.source_id ?? 'default';
    if (sourceId.trim().length === 0) {
      throw new Error('page.source_id must be a non-empty string');
    }
```

`putPage()` uses `page.source_id || 'default'`, which will silently treat an empty-string source id as the default source. That can reintroduce the “writes land under default” class of bugs if an upstream caller accidentally passes `''` (or any other falsy value) instead of a real source id. Prefer nullish-coalescing (`??`) and/or validate that `source_id` is a non-empty string so invalid source IDs fail fast rather than falling back to `default`.
```suggestion
    const rawSourceId = page.source_id;
    if (rawSourceId !== undefined && rawSourceId !== null && (typeof rawSourceId !== 'string' || rawSourceId.trim() === '')) {
      throw new Error('page.source_id must be a non-empty string when provided');
    }
    const sourceId = rawSourceId ?? 'default';
```

`createVersion()` returns `rows[0]` without checking whether the INSERT…SELECT matched a page. When the slug (or slug+source) doesn’t exist, this will return `undefined` while the method is typed as `Promise<PageVersion>`, leading to downstream runtime errors. Mirror the PostgresEngine behavior here by throwing a clear error when `rows.length === 0`.
```suggestion
    );
    if (rows.length === 0) {
      throw new Error(
        sourceId === undefined
          ? `Page not found for slug "${slug}" and source_id "default"`
          : `Page not found for slug "${slug}" and source_id "${sourceId}"`
      );
    }
```
